out 1810 to 1780 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumu-la-el"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumu-la-el.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumu-la-El</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumu-la-El was an early king of the First Dynasty of Babylon, successor to Sumuabum.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumuabum"><span class="enc-name">Sumuabum</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumuabum was an Amorite chieftain who founded the First Dynasty of Babylon around 1894 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumy</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumy is a city in north-eastern Ukraine and the centre of Sumy Oblast, on the Psel river close to the Russian border.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumyafa-ashwa"><span class="enc-name">Sumyafa Ashwa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumyafa Ashwa was a Himyarite Christian installed by the Aksumite king Kaleb as ruler of Himyar in Yemen after the defeat of Yusuf Dhu Nuwas around 525.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-bin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-bin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Bin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Bin was a Chinese military strategist of the Warring States period, said to be a descendant of Sun Wu, author of The Art of War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-ce"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-ce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span clas