href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-quan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Quan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Quan was the founder of the state of Eastern Wu in southern China during the Three Kingdoms period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-tzu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Tzu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Tzu (Sunzi, Sun Wu) is the traditional author of The Art of War, the most famous Chinese work on military strategy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-yat-sen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Yat-sen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Yat-sen was a Chinese revolutionary and founder of the Kuomintang (Nationalist Party).</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/suna-ii"><span class="enc-name">Suna II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Suna II was kabaka of Buganda from about 1832 to 1856.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunan-ampel"><span class="enc-name">Sunan Ampel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sunan Ampel, born Raden Rahmat, was one of the Wali Songo, the nine saints credited with spreading Islam in Java.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunan-giri"><span class="enc-name">Sunan Giri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sunan Giri, born Raden Paku, was one of the Wali Songo, the nine saints credited with spreading Islam in Java.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunan-gunung-j